 $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.1$ | $0.5398$ | $0.5438$ | $0.5478$ | $0.5517$ |\n| $0.2$ | $0.5793$ | $0.5832$ | $0.5871$ | $0.5910$ |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6179$ | $0.6217$ | $0.6255$ | $0.6293$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188560 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45de5135-ee01-5bcd-b1d8-57abf5216ebd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188560 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1061 34668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67ca45ff-80bb-5b36-af8f-70150761b296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189083 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189101 #edb/ref 1061 37958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "08bccdd5-251e-5183-a107-29a4c922e170" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189102 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189102 #edb/ref 1061 37959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "791c1871-8fb1-51ca-a60a-df0bdf72b5f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189373 #edb/ref 1064 "A set of random variables is said to be **independent and identically distributed** (or **I.I.D.**) if the random variables are mutually independent and have the same probability distribution.\n\nWhen we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population, the sample elements\n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad \\ldots,\\quad X_n\n$$\n\nare often considered to be I.I.D. random variables. This assumption often allows us to simplify the statistical analysis. \n\nSuppose that the random variables $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are I.I.D, where\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n.\n$$\n\nLet's consider their sum\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n.\n$$\n\nSince each $X_i$ is normally distributed, it follows that $Y$ is normally distributed as well. Moreover, we can compute the mean and the variance of $Y$ as follows:\n\n- Calculating the mean using the properties of expectation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{E}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{E}[{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\mu +\\mu +⋯+\\mu  \\\\  & =n\\mu .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, using the properties of variance, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}] \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =n{\\sigma }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots+X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nWe will soon use this important result to develop models of how random samples are distributed. But for now, let's practice working with I.I.D normal random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189373 #edb/ref 1065 6260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189373 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6bb107aa-ee09-57fa-9b9a-f4267b123dac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189373 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429275 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429275 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is t